COMPLETE PROCUREMENT ARCHITECTURE — BOARDS 1–6
At this point, the architecture becomes very clean:
BOARD 1 — INVENTORY MASTER & STOCK LEDGER
Item SKU UOM Batch Serial Cost Stock Reorder → → → → → → →
↓
BOARD 2 — WAREHOUSE EXECUTION
Warehouse Zone Bin Receive Put-Away Pick Pack Dispatch → → → → → → →
↓
BOARD 3 — INVENTORY CONTROL
Transfer Count Variance Adjustment Reconciliation → → → →
↓
BOARD 4 — PROCUREMENT INITIATION
Supplier Demand Internal Stock Check PR Approval Consolidation → → → → →
↓
BOARD 5 — SOURCING & COMMERCIAL COMMITMENT
RFQ Quote Compare Negotiate Award PO → → → → →
↓
BOARD 6 — PROCUREMENT COMPLETION
Delivery Inspection GRN Invoice Match Finance Closure → → → → → →
Key recommendation.
I would make one requirement especially explicit in the final SOW:
TICVAI shall implement one continuous Procure-to-Pay transaction chain with shared identifiers and audit 
history, rather than separate PR, RFQ, PO, warehouse, GRN and invoice systems connected only by manual 
references.
For example, from any invoice, an authorized user should be able to navigate backward:
Invoice GRN Receipt PO Award Supplier Quote RFQ Purchase Request Original Demand → → → → → → → →
And forward:

TICVAI / INVENTORY & PROCUREMENT BACKEND STRUCTURE
CONFIDENTIAL | 152
PR RFQ Award PO Delivery GRN Invoice Finance Status Closure → → → → → → → →
That is important for TICVAI because it turns Inventory & Procurement into one integrated enterprise engine 
rather than several disconnected backend modules.

