COMPLETE INVENTORY & PROCUREMENT ARCHITECTURE — BOARDS 1–7
Board Responsibility
Board 1 Inventory Master & Stock Control
Board 2 Warehouse, Location & Fulfilment Operations
Board 3 Transfers, Counts, Adjustments & Reconciliation
Board 4 Supplier & Purchase Request Management
Board 5 RFQ, Sourcing, Quotations & Purchase Orders
Board 6 Receiving, GRN, Invoice Matching & Procurement Closure
Board 7 Planning, Analytics, AI Optimization, Controls & Integration
Architectural recommendation
Board 7 should not become another transactional system. It should sit above Boards 1–6 as TICVAI's Inventory 
& Procurement Intelligence Layer.
The architecture should therefore be:

TICVAI / INVENTORY & PROCUREMENT BACKEND STRUCTURE
CONFIDENTIAL | 175
Transactional Engines — Boards 1–6
↓
Unified Inventory & Procurement Data
↓
BOARD 7 — INTELLIGENCE & CONTROL
Forecast
→ Analyze
→ Detect
→ Recommend
→ Simulate
→ Approve
↓
Send execution back to the correct engine:
Transfer Board 3 →
Warehouse Task Board 2 →
Purchase Request Board 4 →
RFQ / PO Board 5 →
Receiving / Match Board 6 →
This prevents AI from becoming a disconnected chatbot. It makes AI an actionable layer on top of the actual 
TICVAI workflows.
The strongest differentiator here is the connection between ticket demand and inventory/procurement 
planning. TICVAI already knows ticket sales, attendance forecasts, events, F&B demand and retail activity. That 
information should automatically improve inventory forecasts and purchasing decisions.
For an attractions and entertainment platform, that is considerably more valuable than building a traditional 
standalone inventory system.